      "question": "<p>In this question <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\"></span> are positive integers with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q &gt; p\"></span></p><p>Statement 1: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q^3 - p^3\"></span> is never a multiple of 5</p><p>(a) Show, by means of a counter example, that Statement 1 is not true.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></p><p>Statement 2: When <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\"></span> are consecutive even integers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q^3 - p^3\"></span> is a multiple of 8</p><p>(b) Prove, using algebra, that Statement 2 is true.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p>",
